  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "GhjPK0OU6F7MV7efFmIeRmfL64M=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:54:22.913Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Six practices for designing a state schema, from the Mastery Track:\n\n1. **Start simple, iterate** \u2014 begin with the core entities and properties; the schema can expand as the requirements become clear.\n2. **Clarity and explicitness** \u2014 name types and fields descriptively.\n3. **Use non-null wisely** \u2014 enforce integrity with `!` where a value must always exist, but do not over-constrain a field that is genuinely optional.\n4. **Normalize vs denormalize** \u2014 normalizing means distinct types linked by ids (a `TodoItem.assigneeId` pointing at a `User.id`), which reduces redundancy; denormalizing embeds the data where it is used. \"The choice depends on your specific use case, query patterns, and how data is updated.\"\n5. **Consider future needs** without over-engineering \u2014 a `createdAt` or `dueDate` may be worth adding early.\n6. **Root state type** \u2014 a single root type for the document state (`TodoListState`) as a clear entry point.\n\nPractice 3 is the one with a Powerhouse-specific edge: because a document must be creatable **empty**, a root field can only be non-null if it has a sensible default \u2014 an empty list, or an enum with an initial value \u2014 which is why collections are `[Type!]!` and most scalar fields are optional. Practice 4 has one too: an operation is the only way state changes, so a normalized reference means writing an operation that resolves the link, while a denormalized copy means writing an operation that keeps two places in step.\n\nThe page closes on the through-line: \"the schema dictates how data is stored, and how it can be queried and mutated through operations.\"\n\nPractically, the schema is authored in **Vetra Studio** (`ph vetra --watch`) or Connect: name the model (capitalisation matters \u2014 \"it influences our code generation\"), give it a namespaced document type such as `powerhouse/todo-list`, paste the SDL and press **Sync with schema**, which generates the initial JSON state so the shape can be verified before any operation exists.\n\n## Provenance\n- `apps/academy/docs/academy/03-Build/02-DocumentModelCreation/02-SpecifyTheStateSchema.md` \u2014 Best practices for designing your state schema; Practical implementation: defining the state schema in Vetra Studio\n\n_Extracted from the Powerhouse repository at **v6.2.2-dev.74**._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-09-03T07:54:22.913Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:54:22.913Z"
    }
  },
